Z||000000||johnmac||02-26-2001||10:29 PM||||Has anyone used ppg paint - a base with clear cover paint.<P>Suppose to be very good but expensive.<P>I was informed there was a cheaper version of the same paint.<P>Any comments?<P>John MacHaffie||||reg||1
Z||000001||johnmac||02-27-2001||04:22 AM||||I was quoted about $300 for the ppg paint and about $175 for the cheaper "omni" paint. Several car enthusias here in Florida love it.<P>Anyone use it on the Fiero?<P>John||||reg||6
Z||000002||Alex4mula||02-27-2001||08:22 AM||||My car is currently being painted (red) & it suposed to be PPG. Will see the results in about a week.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2xsess||02-27-2001||01:45 PM||||I used to sell Dupont, ppg was my enemy, but they're product is very good as is Duponts of course. And BASF makes good quality product too. A good painter can make just about any paint look great. <P>I've heard of Omni but don't know much about it. I think Dupont might be a bit less expensive than ppg, might be worth checking out.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||02-27-2001||05:13 PM||||OMNI is cheap stuff like MAACO uses. ICI is a division of PPG, its great, i use it on 90% of my jobs. 1 QUART is enough for a complete job with HVLP gun of the basecoat. Im doing Lisas GT with 1 qt of base, 1 qt of clear. 3 coats of each. Less than $200 in material, base, clear, hardner, reducer. They do have 2 prices 2K is the expensive one that has differnt shading of same color, Zyna-Gold is only the OEM colors. I use the Zyna on all allovers. I use the 2K when I have to make a better match on a panel repair. BUT they are the same material. Feel free to email if you need more details.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 02-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||02-27-2001||08:56 PM||||Paint is one area where you pretty much get what you pay for.<P>PPG is the maker of the original paints. The new stuff holds up better.<P>DuPont makes some interesting stuff too. I've always been parcial to Imron but I don't know how much it likes the flexible stuff on the nose and tail. I haven't been around paint in years. <P>main thing is getting every bit of the car sanded then chem preped. you want to remove as much side trim and stuff as you can. Then the new paint will be under the trim when it's put back. This makes the paint less likely to peal along the trim. (It's easier to remove it then try to sand next to it and clean under it etc. Any dirt under the trim could blow out durring painting and crap up the job.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||02-27-2001||09:46 PM||||When we repaint my coupe as soon as it gets above 60 degrees out, I think I am going to spend the extra and get PPG.  We have used DuPont Centari almost exclusivly on all the cars we have painted, but I think my dad has also sprayed PPG before.  Centari is currently on my winter car, and for a single stage its holding up very well, and I, unfortunatly, havent been able to wax it as much as I want because of the cold temps, but it still shines great.<P>Otherwise, Im definatly going with 4 coats base and 6 clear (those being double coats). <P>rogergarrison -<P> My dad was also thinking about HVLP, he has never sprayed it before, but thought if he could find something for the right price, he may try it.  We dont have a completly dustless enviroment and we try our best to keep it down, but I really dont want to have to buff out a whole lot on brand new paint.  What psi does HVLP spray at, and with what consumption.  We have a 60 gallon 5hp compressor.  Is the technique very different from regular painting, to where we'd have to learn how to get it good before we tried it on my car?  Also, I guess it also depends on how much reducer and hardner you are using (for temperature), but you think I should be able to get away with 1 qt of base and clear for my notchback?<P>Thanks in advance!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||2xsess||02-27-2001||10:36 PM||||Imron, how, I didn't think anyone even knew about that stuff.<P>For those of you who might not know, Imron is used mostly on Big Rigs. I've heard of people painting cars with it and having ggod results, the stuff is tough as anything. smells wicked too, yuck!<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||02-28-2001||06:39 AM||||HVLP=high volume low pressure. I have a 6hp 60 gal compressor for my whole shop. The guns are pretty expensive $300-$400. I spray an all over job @ 8 pounds. 98% of the paint goes on the vehicle very little overspray in the air. It takes a little while to adjust to getting a smooth finish at that low a pressure. When I switched all my jobs were very orange peely, but I wet sand and polish all jobs anyway. What I saved in material paid for the gun very quickly, cut my materials at least in half. Imron is a trade name for Dupont-its really just a cataylized acrylic enamel. I tried it when it first came out on my airplane. It didnt impress me that much for the price it cost, only held up slightly longer than acrylic enamel and I dont think its available in basecoat/clrcoats. Pretty sure its only single stage. My feelings on clearcoat is a finished maximum of 2 coats (more if its sanded off and buffed) The more clear, the more chance of cracking and peeling down the road. The idea that the more clear the deeper the shine is a myth. I can polish 2 coats and look better than a job that has 10 coats. This came from the old days when there were as many as 40 coats of laquer. You dont realized that most of it ended up sanded back off to get that glassy smooth surface. Also film thickness of laquer was very tiny per coat. One coat of urathane clear is equal to many coats of laquer. Ive been doing custom paint jobs since 65, metalflake, candy, 'real' pearls, fish scale, lace...u name it.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||02-28-2001||07:39 AM||||Hey Roger,<P>I'm only a few months (I hope) from laying color on my 68 Camaro. I'm planning on using base coat clear coat non-metallic color and I've been eye-balling the HVLP guns. Have you ever tried the cheaper guns? I've been seeing them for sale for around $100 with regulator and gauge.<P>When I got my 69 Firebird painted, the painter used a DeVillbis (sp?) HVLP gun for the metallic paint and a conventional gun for the clear (He said he could make the clear lay down smoother with the high pressure gun). Any thoughts on his technique?<P>By the way, I have a little experience with autopainting but it dates back to the laquer days. I've also done a little acrylic enamel.<P>Looking forward to your opinion,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000010||SteveJ||02-28-2001||09:12 AM||||Home Depot has a gravity fed HVLP gun for $99 which looks interesting.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||02-28-2001||10:31 AM||||Im sure the cheaper guns are probably adequate. Its probably in the material used to make it.....lot of plastic instead of brass, alloy etc. My HVLP gun is almost 10 years old and I still use it every day. <P>As for the old syphon feed guns, like DeVilbiss and Binks, They atomize the paint particals much finer because of the higher pressure. Its a little trickier shooting metalics with HVLP. I still use my Devilbiss for spot repair and blending into an original finish, then use the HVLP to clear the panel. The syphon gun will make for a lot smoother finish but wastes a lot of material. But if you wet sand and rub it thats not that important an issue. If you dont want to wet sand, prob the smoothest way to go is a syphon gun, but plan on using at least twice as much paint. About laquer, its no longer manufactured and only a very few auto paint suppy stores even have any. If they do sell it, its limited to whats already in stock. In most states its now illegal to sell laquer, and the syphon feed guns are almost finished too.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 02-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||SteveJ||02-28-2001||11:37 AM||||Spray gun basics: <A HREF="http://www.autobodypro.com/tektips/articles/spray_guns.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autobodypro.com/tektips/articles/spray_guns.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||opm2000||03-01-2001||12:01 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by johnmac:<BR>[B]Has anyone used ppg paint - a base with clear cover paint.<P>John,<BR>      I can't comment on the clearcoat cause I never had to use it.   Butt...I've shot a boat load of PPG over the last 15 years, onto metal, fiberglass, fabric, and aluminum.   It's a great paint system, but toxic as the dickens.   <P>It will seek your internal body via any moist part of your body, like eyes, nose, skin, hands.....so you must have the spraybooth environment totally under controll.  I use a full body tyveck suit, surgeons gloves, a full face respirator with a remote air sysstem....<P>That said, you must clean the base surface very well, physically or chemically.  Get anal, it pays off.   Then shoot two cross coats DP40 primer.   It sticks to everything like Flint, and is hard as hell to remove.<P>   Then follow up with K200 sanding primer.   Shoot three ccross coats on everything, sand with 80 to 200 grit till the high spots start coming thru.   Then shoot another 2 to 6 coats with a trace color like lamp black.   When you sand these layers, sand till the differently colored high spots start showing thru.   <P>   When you begin to think you are getting the right amount of fill, begin using real fine grades of sandpaper.   You may even use something like 600 on the very last coat.<P>Then shoot a sealer coat of diluted DP40.   This seals out moisture, which is 1/2 of why any paint job begins to fail.<P>Then shoot three cross coats color, or whatever it takes to hide.   If  any dust or bugs get into your paint booth, plan on color sanding.<P>Color sand with 800 to 1000 grit, and then polish with your favorite brand of buffer applied paint polish.   The PPG will shine like a million bucks.   You can see your reflection well enough to shave by it.<P>Being frugal, I use a 5 hp/20 gallon compressor, with two particle filters and two water seperators.   Never spray when the humidity is above 60 0/0.<P>I use a Devilbiss JGA500 conventional gun.   I have three different fluid tips and needels, the spec sheet of your color coat will tell you what you need, at what psi of air.<P>I bought a cheap 2 qt remote spray gun from JCWhitney, thru away the gun that came with it, and put the Devilbiss gun onto it.   Works great.   I can shoot an entire piece, many times with two coats, without having to stop & remix.   It matters when it's hot.<P>I allways get orange peel, but color sand it out easily.   If you get runs or sags, slice them off with a razor blade or carefully sand them off.   Then color sand entire panel with 600 or finer, and shoot one ccoat color.   Most times the one coat will come out smooth & shiney , without color sanding.<P>I spent most of last summer shooting a huge fiberglass homebuilt airplane, piece by piece.   It looks better than my wife, Mercedes.<P>David Breeze <P>||||reg||7
Z||000014||gabriel||03-02-2001||06:37 AM||||Wow!  Is all that cross spraying of primer needed if the base paint is in good condition?  I can understand if the surface is bare...<P>&gt;I use a full body tyveck suit, surgeons gloves, a full face respirator with a remote air sysstem....<BR> <BR>  Don't think I'll be messing with that paint anytime soon. -As in this lifetime ... Remote air system?<P>All that sounds quite *expensive* for someone that doesn't use it everday.<P> &gt;It looks better than my wife, Mercedes.<P>  Hope she doesn't ever read that!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
